From b3dd2b8a0242519ca7cdec3f9413561d52263518 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noel Blatter <noel.blatter@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 21:28:09 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Forward pass: no zero terms in the dual numbers, noise sums
 without full arrays

Dual numbers: most operations in the component formulas combine a Dual
with a plain number or array, which has no derivative. The product rule
then needs one term and a sum leaves the derivative as it is; so far
both computed and added an array of zeros. Values are the same, only the
sign of a zero can differ.

Noise sums: N y and N^H y have few entries. They are scattered straight
into the second right-hand side, and the noise power y^H N y is summed
over the noise entries. Two arrays (F, m), their sum and a conjugated
copy of Y are gone. The right-hand side is bit-identical, the power is
summed in a different order.

Public fixture: carrier, signal and powers bit-identical, noise 6.3e-16,
sensitivity 4.1e-16, gradient 1.6e-14 relative, error against the
reference unchanged. All scripts under runs/philip and runs/flags pass,
also PASS on emulated x86-64 with the native factorization (fixture and
five variants).

Apple M4, host callbacks: entries with derivatives 2.33 -> 1.96 ms,
forward callback 17.3 -> 16.8 ms. benchmark.py, alternating runs, median:
forward phase 22.0 -> 21.1 ms (macOS, 14 runs), 27.5 -> 26.3 ms (Docker,
Linux aarch64, one CPU, 16 runs; the gradient phase there 5.4 -> 5.7 ms).
Not measured on the Xeon. Shipped bytecode of fast.py regenerated.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 differometor/__pycache__/fast.cpython-312.pyc | Bin 109169 -> 109728 bytes
 differometor/fast.py                          |  67 +++++++++++-------
 2 files changed, 41 insertions(+), 26 deletions(-)

